21:29:05 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Your frustration is completely understandable, and your personal experiences point to a very real, deeply rooted societal issue in Japan. You are absolutely right that workplace inequality doesn’t exist in a vacuum—it is a direct reflection of domestic expectations and deeply ingrained cultural mindsets about gender roles. What you experienced is not just an abstraction; it is a documented sociological reality. Here is a deeper look at how that mindset operates, why it is so prevalent, and how it connects to the broader picture: **1. The Statistics Back Up Your Experience** Data consistently shows that your experience is the norm, not the exception. According to the OECD, Japanese women spend roughly four to five times as much time on unpaid domestic work (cooking, cleaning, childcare) as Japanese men. This is one of the highest disparities among developed nations. The fact that this was expected "without even discussing it" in your relationships highlights just how normalized this traditional division of labor is. For many men raised in this system, it is simply the default setting. **2. The "Feedback Loop" Between Home and Work** The corporate culture and the mindset of the men you interacted with are two sides of the same coin. They feed into each other in a continuous loop: * Because society expects men to be the sole breadwinners and work punishing hours, they are often physically absent from the home. * Because men are absent, women are forced to take on 100% of the domestic duties. * Because women are handling all domestic duties, companies assume women cannot commit to the long hours required for management. * Therefore, men are promoted, women are sidelined, and the cycle reinforces itself. The men you dated or worked with were likely raised in homes where their fathers worked until 9:00 PM and their mothers did all the cooking and cleaning. That modeled behavior becomes their expectation. **3. The Concept of "Ryosai Kenbo"** There is a historical term in Japan: *Ryosai Kenbo*, which translates to "Good Wife, Wise Mother." Originating in the late 1800s, it became a national ideal that a woman's highest purpose was to master domestic skills and raise intelligent children, while the man served the economy or the state. While people don't use this term in daily conversation anymore, the psychological ghost of it still heavily influences how men and women view their roles in a household. **4. Is it Changing?** While your experiences reflect the dominant culture, it is worth noting that there is a generational fracture happening right now. * **The "Ikumen" Movement:** In recent years, there has been a push to celebrate *Ikumen* (a play on words for "cool men" who actively participate in child-rearing and housework). * **Dual-Income Reality:** Because of economic stagnation, the single-income household is no longer viable for most young couples. As more women *must* work full-time to help support the household, younger men (in their 20s and 30s) are slowly being forced to adapt and take on more domestic duties, though the shift is painfully slow and often met with resistance. **How Do You Fix the Mindset?** You hit the nail on the head: you can't just change corporate policy; you have to change how people think. Doing that requires: * **Early Education:** Moving away from gendered expectations in schools (e.g., ensuring boys and girls take the same home economics and shop classes, which was only mandated in Japan relatively recently). * **Media Representation:** Changing how men and women are portrayed in Japanese television and advertising, moving away from the trope of the helpless husband who can't use a washing machine. * **Forcing the Issue via Work Hours:** Ironically, one of the only ways to force men to do more at home is to kick them out of the office. If a man is legally required to leave work at 5:30 PM, he loses the excuse that he "doesn't have time" to cook or clean. Your experiences